<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Pellene</h1><p class="st-meta">800 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.) · capital Pellene</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Easternmost city of Achaea, a loyal Spartan ally famous for the woollen cloaks given as prizes at its games.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Pellene stood on a high hill in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a>, near the border with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sicyon">Sicyon</a>, a short way inland from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-corinth">Gulf of Corinth</a>. It was one of the twelve Achaean cities, formed in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Pellene was known for its warm woollen cloaks, awarded as prizes at its local games, and for its sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>. Unlike most Achaean cities, it sided with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sparta">Sparta</a> at the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a>. In the 4th century BCE it was ruled at times by tyrants, including the wrestler Chaeron, who was installed with Macedonian backing.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Pellene joined the revived <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/achaean-league">Achaean League</a>. In 241 BCE the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/aetolian-league">Aetolian League</a> sacked it, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aratus-of-sicyon">Aratus</a> routed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolians">Aetolians</a> in its streets. It passed under Roman control in 146 BCE and later dwindled.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/pellene.jpg" alt="Pellene" width="320" height="175"><figcaption>Image: Badseed · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Towns_of_ancient_Achaia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pellene</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Chaeron</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion; cult of Athena</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Achaean Doric Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Greece</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Pellana</dd></dl></aside></div>

